# `@template` - marks a definition written to be quoted (design syntax.md
# G7): a tree whose code may never run, read through `name::$ast`.
#
# `@template` is predefined (wyrm_eval_parse_tree's native decorators), so
# no import is needed. This module is the same decorator written in wyrm,
# kept so that `import wyrm::template::*` still works:
#
#     @template
#     fn $tmpl(e):
#         this ! peek(0)
#
# The decorator answers the definition wrapped in an annotation,
# `(annotate template (true) <definition>)`, for the compiler to read. A
# compiler lowers the definition as usual, and if lowering fails it emits a
# trap in its place instead of an error. This interpreter never lowers
# anything, so for it the annotation changes nothing: the definition runs
# as written, and `name::$ast` is its tree without the annotation.

fn [TreeBase] template():
    return $['annotate, 'template, $['true], sexpr(this)]
